    {:arglists ([f]),
     :doc
     "Returns a function, which will install the same bindings in effect as in\n  the thread at the time bound-fn* was called and then call f with any given\n  arguments. This may be used to define a helper function which runs on a\n  different thread, but needs the same bindings in place."},
    bound?
    {:arglists ([& vars]),
     :doc
     "Returns true if all of the vars provided as arguments have any bound value, root or thread-local.\n   Implies that deref'ing the provided vars will succeed. Returns true if no vars are provided."},
    bounded-count
    {:arglists ([n coll]),
     :doc
     "If coll is counted? returns its count, else will count at most the first n\n  elements of coll using its seq"},
    butlast
    {:arglists ([coll]),
     :doc
     "Return a seq of all but the last item in coll, in linear time."},
    byte {:arglists ([x]), :doc "Coerce to byte"},
    byte-array
    {:arglists ([size-or-seq] [size init-val-or-seq]),
     :doc "Creates an array of bytes"},
    bytes? {:arglists ([x]), :doc "Return true if x is a byte array"},
    case
    {:arglists ([e & clauses]),
     :doc
     "Takes an expression, and a set of clauses.\n\n   Each clause can take the form of either:\n\n   test-constant result-expr\n\n   (test-constant1 ... test-constantN)  result-expr\n\n   The test-constants are not evaluated. They must be compile-time\n   literals, and need not be quoted.  If the expression is equal to a\n   test-constant, the corresponding result-expr is returned. A single\n   default expression can follow the clauses, and its value will be\n   returned if no clause matches. If no default expression is provided\n   and no clause matches, an IllegalArgumentException is thrown.\n\n   Unlike cond and condp, case does a constant-time dispatch, the\n   clauses are not considered sequentially.  All manner of constant\n   expressions are acceptable in case, including numbers, strings,\n   symbols, keywords, and (Clojure) composites thereof. Note that since\n   lists are used to group multiple constants that map to the same\n   expression, a vector can be used to match a list if needed. The\n   test-constants need not be all of the same type.",
     :added "1.2",
     :macro true},
    case-hash
    {:arglists ([input]),
     :doc
     "Returns the input if it is within the range of a 32-bit signed integer, otherwise returns the hash of the\n  input. The native hash returns a int32 int so this is to make sure that (case-hash (case-hash x)) == (case-hash x).\n  A key may be hashed more than once and we need to make sure its value does not change in later hashing."},
    case-map
    {:arglists
     ([expr-sym default case-f test-f tests thens skip-check])},
    case-map-collison-merged
    {:arglists ([case-f test-f tests thens]),
     :doc
     "Transforms a sequence of test constants and their corresponding branch expressions\n   into a sorted map for consumption by `case*`.\n\n   This version is selected when hash collisions have been detected (i.e. the skip-check set is nonempty)\n   and resolved (typically via merging in `merge-hash-collisions`). In these cases, the branch expressions\n   have already been pre-wrapped in `condp` forms to handle multiple colliding constants. Therefore, no additional\n   wrapping is needed here-the transformed test constants are mapped directly to their corresponding branch expressions.\n\n   Returns a sorted map mapping each transformed test constant directly to its branch expression."},
    case-map-with-check
    {:arglists ([expr-sym default case-f test-f tests thens]),
     :doc
     "Transforms a sequence of test constants and their corresponding branch expressions\n   into a sorted map for consumption by `case*`. This version is used when no hash collisions\n   are detected (i.e. the skip-check set is empty).\n\n   Returns a sorted map where each key is the transformed test constant and each value is a `condp` form\n   that checks the original expression against the test constant before selecting the branch."},
